STILL AN ASSUMPTION
  The two-leg default. A strategy that EXITS AS A MAKER pays nothing on the way
  out, so its true hurdle is the one-leg figure. We charge two legs unless a
  strategy sets holds_to_resolution, which over-states the hurdle for any
  liquidity-providing exit. Conservative, but not measured.

ROUND 24 AUDIT - ORDINARY INCOME WAS REPORTED BUT NEVER RESERVED
  `calculate_tax_summary` computed `net_ordinary_income` and returned it for the
  Obsidian card, then built `tax_escrow_reserve` from short- and long-term
  capital gains ONLY. Funding accruals are IRC 61 ordinary income and the tax is
  really owed, so a basis harvester earning funding all year had its entire tax
  bill handed back to it as deployable bankroll. Measured on a probe ledger:
  $5,000 of funding income produced $0.00 of escrow and $9,965 of "safe"
  bankroll against $1,785 genuinely owed.

  Now escrowed at the composite ordinary rate, floored at zero, and broken out
  as escrow_short_term / escrow_long_term / escrow_ordinary so the total is
  auditable. Capital losses do NOT offset it: IRC 1211 caps that at $3,000/yr
  for an individual and applying it would move the reserve DOWN on an
  assumption.

ROUND 25 - SECTION 1256 60/40 IS NOW APPLIED (OPERATOR-AUTHORISED)
  Futures P&L is carved OUT of the short/long term buckets and escrowed at
  0.60*lt_rate + 0.40*st_rate. With the configured rates that is 26.0%, versus
  35% before - $900 released per $10,000 of futures gains.

  This LOWERS the reserve, so it was held back in Round 24 and applied only on
  an explicit instruction. Three guards, all tested:
    - futures are removed from st_net/lt_net, so they are not taxed twice;
    - a net 1256 LOSS reserves nothing and releases nothing (it carries back
      three years against prior 1256 gains - a filing election, not cash today);
    - a futures loss does not offset a crypto gain.

  26.0%, NOT the 27.2% quoted in the Round 25 brief. The difference is that
  `lt_rate` carries no safety_buffer_pct while `st_rate` does; 27.2% assumes a
  buffered LT leg. That asymmetry predates 1256 and was NOT changed here,
  because doing so would silently re-rate every long-term gain in the ledger.
  Decide whether the buffer belongs on lt_rate, then this follows.

STILL NOT MODELLED - SECTION 1256 YEAR-END MARK-TO-MARKET
  Open 1256 positions on Dec 31 are deemed sold at fair value and the gain is
  taxable with NO closing trade to trigger it. Nothing models this, and unlike
  the 60/40 split it UNDER-reserves: carry open CME contracts across a year end
  and the escrow will not know about the gain. Needs a year-end position
  snapshot, which the ledger does not currently take.

